_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97418" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-97418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97418" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2t+\\sqrt{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+\\sqrt{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{8{t}^{2}\\sqrt{t}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{103}{30}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{5}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\frac{103}{30}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-((-\\pi )+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi +1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi +2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-141170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=3t+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+3+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{7}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }((2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(6\\pi +0+0)-(3\\pi +0+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\sqrt{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3\\pi +5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{9\\pi +5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18+8+9 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12+1+3 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{16}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=1-\\sqrt{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(1-2\\sqrt{t}+t),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t-\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\sqrt{t})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3(1-\\sqrt{t})"] ["math" "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{t}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(1-\\sqrt{t})"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-43926" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{6}(1-\\sqrt{t})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{50}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(2+t)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}(2+t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-42741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=2-3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2-3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(4-12t+9{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(4t-6{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4-6+3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3t}{1} \\\\  & =2-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6b3dde96a478b61d719fd518dcbfe870937305ab4a3d5e1e2ed43ec4aebbdce6"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-3t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}(2-3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(2-3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2-3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(3t-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "q-97721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/state.json" "file_sha256" "b8eb6d9e26d862ba71181b65bd1572d54a9581dc2b38faadc8fb0aaebd83ddcb"} {"question" "e-5908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5908.json" "file_sha256" "57d24c849b65e5b8ac34183544542d28f9521221420b2bda7d0c1382d4314a97"} {"question" "e-5908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5908.json" "file_sha256" "57d24c849b65e5b8ac34183544542d28f9521221420b2bda7d0c1382d4314a97"} {"question" "e-5548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector  $p(t) = 1 - t^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5548.json" "file_sha256" "d3bf8037c9d3835033d7d12487ecd7375ecce85e92b94e1596511c259c249f20"} {"question" "e-5548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${p}(t) = 1-t^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad \np(2)= -3,\\qquad \np(3)=-8.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+9+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{73}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5548.json" "file_sha256" "d3bf8037c9d3835033d7d12487ecd7375ecce85e92b94e1596511c259c249f20"} {"question" "e-5549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5549.json" "file_sha256" "f6b4d361bcba77e4ddcff95327e65692e07239e28bbac8257a53c88388fdae68"} {"question" "e-5549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}-2{t}^{2}+t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5549.json" "file_sha256" "f6b4d361bcba77e4ddcff95327e65692e07239e28bbac8257a53c88388fdae68"} {"question" "e-5550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5550.json" "file_sha256" "a336f55ef818104f3608c1e61c0dc7eaef28dade692fcebd45d10c080389f2e1"} {"question" "e-5550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle  f(t),  f(t) \\rangle}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }9{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\|  = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi }}\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{\\pi \\sqrt{\\pi }}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040608/example-5550.json" "file_sha256" "a336f55ef818104f3608c1e61c0dc7eaef28dade692fcebd45d10c080389f2e1"}] "usage" {"e-5550" [] "e-5908" [] "e-5549" [] "q-97420" [] "e-5548" [] "q-43925" [] "q-42714" [] "q-42741" []} "basis" 1450 "retractions" [[17592186134357 :question/problem "An inner product on $V = R^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [17592186134325 :question/problem "An inner product on $V = R^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 5x_{3}y_{3}$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [17592186134475 :question/problem "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(0)y(0) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 4 - 2t + t^{2}$."] [17592186134329 :question/problem "Let $V = R_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2)$.\nNormalize the vector $p(t) = 2 + t$."] [17592186133552 :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product."] [17592186133552 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖v‖ = \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖v‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨v, v⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3) + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot (1 \\cdot 1)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{18 + 4 + 3} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133497 :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - t^2$."] [17592186133497 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖ = \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{p}(t) = 1-t^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\qquad p(2)= -3,\\qquad p(3)=-8\n$$\n\nComputing $\\|p(t)\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖p(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{⟨p(t), p(t)⟩} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{p(1)p(1) + p(2)p(2) + p(3)p(3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2} + [p(2)]^{2} + [p(3)]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(0)^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (-8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 9 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{73}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133498 :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)= 2t-1$."] [17592186133498 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(2t - 1)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(4t^{2} - 4t + 1)dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4t^{3}}{3} - 2t^{2} + t) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 2 + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{3}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$"] [17592186133499 :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)= 3t$."] [17592186133499 :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\| f(t)\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle f(t), f(t) \\rangle}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(t), f(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{π}(3t)^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}9t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 3π^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| f(t) \\| = \\sqrt{3\\pi^3} = \\pi \\sqrt{3\\pi}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t)$, we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $\\| f(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= (f(t))/(‖f(t)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π\\sqrt{3π}} \\cdot 3t \\\\\n&= \\frac{t\\sqrt{3}}{π\\sqrt{π}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_an